 dict(slug="hepatitis-a-and-e", q="What are hepatitis A and E, and how long does the jaundice last?", seo="Hepatitis A and E: how long does the jaundice last?", cond=5,
  desc="Hepatitis A and E, the commonest causes of jaundice in India: how they spread, symptoms, recovery time, diet, danger signs, pregnancy risk and the hepatitis A vaccine. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="Hepatitis A and E are liver infections spread through contaminated water and food. They are the commonest cause of jaundice in India, especially in the monsoon. Most people recover fully in 4 to 8 weeks with rest and normal food. Hepatitis E can be dangerous in pregnancy, and confusion, bleeding or repeated vomiting need hospital care at once.",
  sections=[
   ("Symptoms", ["- A few days of fever, tiredness, loss of appetite, nausea and vomiting","- Then yellow eyes and skin, dark urine and sometimes pale stools","- Pain or heaviness under the right ribs","- Itching in some people",
     "Children often have mild or no symptoms. Adults, especially older adults and people with existing liver disease, tend to be more unwell."]),
   ("How it is confirmed", ["Liver function tests show raised SGPT, SGOT and bilirubin. Blood tests for IgM anti-HAV (hepatitis A) and IgM anti-HEV (hepatitis E) confirm the cause. Clotting (INR) is checked to see how well the liver is working. An ultrasound rules out a blocked bile duct."]),
   ("Treatment and recovery", ["- There is no specific medicine; the liver heals on its own in most people","- Rest as needed, normal home food, and plenty of safe fluids","- Avoid alcohol, unprescribed medicines, herbal remedies and painkillers in excess",
     "Jaundice usually fades over 4 to 8 weeks. Liver tests can take a few months to become fully normal. A small number develop acute liver failure, which needs hospital care and sometimes a liver transplant."]),
   ("Danger signs, pregnancy and prevention", ["Go to hospital straight away for confusion or unusual sleepiness, bleeding or bruising, vomiting that stops you keeping fluids down, or jaundice that keeps deepening after two weeks. Hepatitis E in the last three months of pregnancy can be severe: any pregnant woman with jaundice should see a doctor the same day.",
     "- Drink boiled or filtered water, and avoid cut fruit, golgappe and street juices in the monsoon","- Wash hands with soap before eating and after the toilet","- Hepatitis A vaccine protects children and adults who are not immune; ask your doctor","- People with fatty liver, hepatitis B or C, or cirrhosis should ask about the hepatitis A vaccine"]),
  ]),
 dict(slug="gilberts-syndrome", q="Bilirubin slightly high but I feel fine: is it Gilbert's syndrome?", seo="Slightly high bilirubin: is it Gilbert's syndrome?", cond=5,
  desc="Gilbert's syndrome: why bilirubin is mildly high with normal liver enzymes, why eyes turn yellow during fasting or illness, how it is confirmed, and why it is harmless. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="Gilbert's syndrome is a common, harmless inherited condition in which the liver processes bilirubin a little slowly. Bilirubin, mostly the indirect type, is mildly raised while all other liver tests are normal. The eyes may look slightly yellow during fasting, illness, stress or poor sleep. It needs no treatment and does not damage the liver.",
  sections=[
   ("Signs", ["- Mildly raised bilirubin, usually under 3 to 4 mg/dL, found on a routine test","- Indirect (unconjugated) bilirubin makes up most of it","- Normal SGPT, SGOT, alkaline phosphatase, albumin and blood counts","- Slightly yellow eyes after fasting, a fever, hard exercise, stress or late nights",
     "It is often first noticed in teenagers and young adults, more often in men, and sometimes during a Navratri or Ramzan fast."]),
   ("How it is confirmed", ["Your doctor checks that the raised bilirubin is mainly indirect, that liver enzymes and blood counts are normal, and that there is no breakdown of red blood cells (haemolysis). Repeating the test after a few weeks usually shows the same mild pattern. A genetic test is rarely needed."]),
   ("What it means for you", ["- It is not a liver disease and does not lead to cirrhosis","- No diet, medicine or 'jaundice treatment' is needed","- Eat regular meals and avoid long fasts if the yellow colour bothers you","- Tell doctors you have it, as a few medicines, including some cancer and HIV medicines, are handled differently"]),
   ("When it is not Gilbert's", ["See a doctor if bilirubin is high along with raised liver enzymes, dark urine, pale stools, itching, anaemia, weight loss or belly pain. These suggest another cause that needs tests."]),
  ]),
 dict(slug="herbal-supplement-liver-damage", q="Can herbal, Ayurvedic or gym supplements damage the liver?", cond=5,
  desc="Liver damage from herbal, Ayurvedic, weight-loss and body-building supplements in India: which products, warning signs, what to do, and how to stay safe. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="Yes. 'Natural' does not always mean safe. In India, herbal and Ayurvedic products, weight-loss and immunity supplements, and body-building products are a growing cause of liver injury, sometimes severe. Most people recover once the product is stopped, but some develop liver failure. Tell your doctor about every supplement you take.",
  sections=[
   ("Products linked to liver injury", ["- Concentrated herbal extracts sold for weight loss, immunity or energy; in some reports these include green tea extract, garcinia, giloy and ashwagandha","- Unlabelled or mixed Ayurvedic preparations, some of which have been found to contain heavy metals such as lead or mercury","- Body-building supplements and anabolic steroids","- 'Liver tonics' and detox products, which can themselves harm the liver",
     "The same product can be safe for one person and harmful for another. Products bought online or without a label carry the highest risk."]),
   ("Warning signs", ["- Loss of appetite, nausea and unusual tiredness","- Yellow eyes, dark urine, itching","- Pain in the upper right belly","- Raised SGPT or SGOT on a routine test after starting a new product"]),
   ("What to do", ["Stop the product and see a doctor. Take the product, its box or a photo of the label with you. Liver tests, tests for viral hepatitis and an ultrasound are done to rule out other causes. Most people improve over weeks to months. Steroids are sometimes used, and a few severe cases need hospital care or a liver transplant. Never restart a product that injured your liver."]),
   ("Staying safe", ["- Ask your doctor before starting any supplement, especially if you have liver disease, take regular medicines or drink alcohol","- Avoid products with no clear ingredient list or licence number","- Do not take several supplements together","- Remember that a balanced diet and exercise do not need supplements for most people"]),
  ]),
 dict(slug="wilsons-disease", q="What is Wilson's disease?", cond=6,
  desc="Wilson's disease, an inherited copper build-up that affects the liver and brain in young people: symptoms, tests, lifelong treatment, diet and family screening. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="Wilson's disease is an inherited condition in which copper builds up in the liver, brain and eyes. It usually shows up between the ages of 5 and 35 as unexplained liver disease, tremor, slurred speech or behaviour change. It is treatable with lifelong medicines that remove copper, and brothers and sisters must be tested.",
  sections=[
   ("Symptoms", ["- Liver: raised liver enzymes, jaundice, fluid in the belly, or cirrhosis at a young age; sometimes sudden liver failure","- Brain: tremor, stiffness, slurred speech, drooling or clumsy handwriting","- Mood and behaviour changes, or falling school performance","- A golden-brown ring around the coloured part of the eye (Kayser-Fleischer ring)"]),
   ("Who is at risk", ["It is inherited from both parents, who usually have no symptoms. It is more common when parents are related by blood. Any young person with unexplained liver disease should be checked for it."]),
   ("Tests", ["Blood ceruloplasmin (usually low), a 24-hour urine copper test (usually high), an eye examination with a slit lamp for Kayser-Fleischer rings, and genetic testing. Sometimes a liver biopsy measures the copper in the liver."]),
   ("Treatment", ["- Lifelong medicines that remove copper or block its absorption, taken every day without breaks","- A low-copper diet, especially in the first year","- Regular blood and urine tests to check copper levels and side effects","- Liver transplant for sudden liver failure or advanced cirrhosis",
     "Stopping treatment can be dangerous. With regular treatment, most people lead a normal life. Brothers, sisters and children of the patient should be tested, even without symptoms."]),
  ]),
 dict(slug="how-much-alcohol-is-safe-for-liver", q="How much alcohol is safe for the liver?", cond=6,
  desc="How much alcohol is safe for the liver: why no amount is fully safe, what a 'drink' means in pegs and bottles, who must not drink at all, myths, and tests. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="No amount of alcohol is completely safe, and the risk to the liver rises with every drink. Drinking every day, binge drinking at weekends, being a woman, being overweight, or having fatty liver or hepatitis B or C all raise the risk. People with any liver disease should not drink at all. Liver damage is silent until late, so check-ups matter.",
  sections=[
   ("What counts as one drink", ["- One 30 ml peg of whisky, rum or vodka","- One small 330 ml bottle of regular beer; a 650 ml bottle is about two, and strong beer more","- One glass (about 150 ml) of wine",
     "Each has roughly 10 to 14 grams of alcohol. Pours at home and at parties are often double this."]),
   ("Who should not drink at all", ["- Anyone with fatty liver, hepatitis B or C, cirrhosis or a past episode of alcoholic hepatitis or pancreatitis","- Pregnant women","- People taking medicines that interact with alcohol, including some TB medicines","- Teenagers"]),
   ("Common myths", ["- 'Beer is light, so it is safe': the alcohol is the same, only diluted","- 'I only drink at weekends': four or five drinks in one sitting harms the liver more than spreading them out","- 'Good-quality whisky does not harm the liver': the liver reacts to alcohol, not the brand","- 'Liver tonics protect the liver': they do not, and some harm it"]),
   ("Checking your liver, and cutting down", ["If you drink regularly, ask for liver tests, an ultrasound and a FibroScan. The liver can recover a lot when drinking stops early. If you find it hard to cut down, ask your doctor about de-addiction support. People who drink heavily every day should not stop suddenly without medical advice, as withdrawal can be dangerous."]),
  ]),
 dict(slug="ultrasound-report-terms", q="What do the words in my ultrasound report mean?", cond=3,
  desc="Ultrasound report terms explained: hepatomegaly, coarse echotexture, bulky pancreas, gallbladder sludge, liver haemangioma, simple cyst, splenomegaly and a dilated bile duct. Dr. Kshitij Lochab, Gurugram.",
  short="Ultrasound reports use words that can sound alarming. Many findings, such as a liver haemangioma, a simple liver cyst or a slightly bulky pancreas, are common and harmless. Others, such as coarse liver texture, an enlarged spleen or a dilated bile duct, need follow-up. Always read the report together with your symptoms and blood tests.",
  sections=[
   ("Liver", ["- Fatty liver or 'increased echogenicity': fat in the liver; see our page on fatty liver grades","- Hepatomegaly: the liver is larger than average; often from fatty liver, sometimes from infection or other causes","- Coarse echotexture or irregular surface: may mean long-standing liver damage or cirrhosis, and needs blood tests and a FibroScan","- Haemangioma: a common, harmless knot of blood vessels; usually needs no treatment","- Simple cyst: a fluid bubble, harmless when it is 'simple' (thin wall, clear fluid)"]),
   ("Gallbladder and bile ducts", ["- Gallbladder sludge: thick bile, seen after fasting, in pregnancy or with rapid weight loss; often clears on its own but can cause pain or pancreatitis","- Contracted or poorly seen gallbladder: often just means you had eaten before the scan","- Thick gallbladder wall: can be from inflammation, liver disease or, rarely, cancer; your doctor decides on further tests","- Dilated or prominent bile duct (CBD): can be normal after gallbladder removal or with age, but with jaundice or abnormal liver tests it needs MRCP or EUS"]),
   ("Pancreas and spleen", ["- Bulky pancreas: often a normal variation, especially in young people; it matters only with pain or raised lipase","- Poorly seen pancreas: common, because gas in the bowel hides it","- Splenomegaly: an enlarged spleen; causes include infections such as malaria, liver disease with high pressure in the portal vein, and blood disorders","- Free fluid: fluid in the belly (ascites), which always needs the cause found"]),
   ("What to do next", ["Take the report to your doctor along with your liver tests. Many findings need nothing more. Findings that usually need a specialist review: coarse liver texture, a liver lump that is not a simple cyst or typical haemangioma, a dilated bile duct with jaundice, splenomegaly, free fluid, or a gallbladder polyp of 6 mm or more."]),
  ]),
